==Trivia==
* Hideyoshi often having {{p|Chimchar}} as his default Pokémon, and sharing Perfect Links with its evolved forms, is likely in reference to real-life {{wp|Toyotomi Hideyoshi}}'s nickname, Kozaru, meaning "little monkey".
* Hideyoshi's rank III skill "Great Uniter" is likely a reference to his real-life counterpart's title as the second "Great Unifier" of Japan.
* Hideyoshi and [[Mitsuhide]] have special quotes when battling each other in a kingdom with banners. Their Pokémon also get maximum energy when the quotes are shown. This is probably a reference to the Battle of Yamazaki, after {{wp|Akechi Mitsuhide}} betrayed and killed {{wp|Oda Nobunaga}}.

===Historical origin===
Hideyoshi is based on the real-life {{wp|Toyotomi Hideyoshi}} of Japanese history. Hideyoshi was originally a servant of [[Nobunaga]]'s who rose to power in his army, succeeding Nobunaga after his death in 1582. [[Nene]] was his first wife, [[Chacha]] ({{wp|Yodo-dono}}) was his concubine and second wife, and [[Maa]] ([[wp:Maeda Toshiie#Family|Ma'a]]) was a concubine. [[Hanbei]] and [[Kanbei]] were Hideyoshi's strategists. [[Hideyori]] ({{wp|Toyotomi Hideyori}}) was his and Chacha's son, and [[Hideaki]] ({{wp|Kobayakawa Hideaki}}) was a nephew and adopted son. [[Takakage]] ({{wp|Kobayakawa Takakage}}), [[Motonari]]'s son, was one of Hideyoshi's retainers.

